notes               = "Untitled in source. The dedication با یاد دوستم ایرج زند ('in memory of my friend Iraj Zand', ۱۳۱۴-۱۳۸۶ / 1935-2007, the painter) is printed in the outer margin, and the date line names the occasion: زمستان ۱۳۸۵ / آخرین نمایشگاه ایرج زند در گالری گلستان/ فرشته‌های شفاف — 'Winter 1385 / Iraj Zand's last exhibition at Golestan Gallery / Transparent Angels'. CRITICAL FORMATTING NOTE: the printed page sets this poem in TWO COLUMNS, which is Persian prosody for a rhymed couplet — the right column holds each first hemistich and the left the second, so line 1 reads تا به گلستان نرسی روح رها نمی‌شود. It is ONE poem, not two, and the transcription joins each pair with a slash. Do not read the columns as separate poems. THE POEM IS BUILT OUT OF THE EXHIBITION: گلستان is at once the rose-garden and the Golestan Gallery where the show hung, and شفافی and فرشته‌های تو are the show's own title. قاصدک is the dandelion clock and also a messenger. دس به عصا is printed in the colloquial contraction of دست به عصا, hand on the cane, i.e. treading carefully. Winter is months 10-12 → +622 = 2007. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

تا به گلستان نرسی / روح رها نمی‌شود
این همه شفافی تو / قسمت ما نمی‌شود
اشک فرشته‌های تو / قاصدکِ رهایی‌اند
تا به مژه نَشسته‌اند / شور به پا نمی‌شود
کاش که شور می‌شدم / شوق و سرور می‌شدم
مثل تو و فرشته‌ات / یک‌شبه نور می‌شدم
گرچه که بندهای ما / یک‌شبه وا نمی‌شود
وین رَهِ پرنشیب را / دس به عصا نمی‌شود
این همه آزادی تو / قسمتِ ما نمی‌شود
تا به گلستان نَرَسی / روح رها نمی‌شود
زمستان ۱۳۸۵
آخرین نمایشگاه ایرج زند در گالری گلستان/ فرشته‌های شفاف

===machine===

Until you reach the rose-garden / the soul is not set free.
All this transparency of yours / is not our portion.
The tears of your angels / are the dandelion-clocks of release;
while they still sit on the lash / no rapture gets to its feet.
If only I could turn to fervour / turn to longing and to joy,
like you and your angel / turn to light in a single night.
Though the bonds on us / do not come open in a night,
and this steep road / is no road for a hand on a stick.
All this freedom of yours / is not our portion.
Until you reach the rose-garden / the soul is not set free.
Winter 1385
Iraj Zand's last exhibition at Golestan Gallery / *Transparent Angels*

===lantern===

Until you reach the rose-garden, the soul is not set free.
All this transparency of yours is not our portion.
The tears of your angels are the dandelion-clocks of release;
while they still sit on the lash, no rapture gets to its feet.
If only I could turn to fervour, turn to longing and to joy,
and like you and your angel turn to light in a single night.
Though the bonds on us do not come open in one night,
and this steep road is no road for a hand on a stick.
All this freedom of yours is not our portion.
Until you reach the rose-garden, the soul is not set free.

Winter 1385
Iraj Zand's last exhibition at Golestan Gallery / Transparent Angels

===translation===

===footnotes===

Iraj Zand died in ۱۳۸۶. The poem is dated from his last show, which was called *Transparent
Angels*, and it is made almost entirely out of that title: the transparency, the angels, and their
tears are the paintings on the wall.

گلستان is a rose-garden and also the name of the gallery the show hung in, so the line *until you
reach the rose-garden the soul is not set free* names the room the poem was written in and the
paradise it is asking about, in one word.

On the printed page the poem is set in two columns — the Persian way of laying out rhymed couplets,
first half-line on the right, second on the left. It reads across, not down.
